Looking at the fflib_SObjectDomain framework: validation hooks are `onValidate()`, and the `error()` helper signatures are `error(String msg)`, `error(String msg, SObject record)` and `error(String msg, SObject record, SObjectField field)` — the 3-arg form puts the error on a specific field. Errors added via the helper are collected in `fflib_SObjectDomain.Errors` and throw a consolidated `fflib_SObjectDomain.WarningException`/Error at the end of the validation phase, only for records that have errors, so other records in the same DML still save. For rule 2 I compare old vs. new: `getChangedRecords()` gives me maps of old→new (also `getOriginalMap()`), letting me detect a pre-update `Closed Won` whose amount changed, while excluding records that transition into Closed Won in this same update. `onValidate` runs for insert (afterApply? no — validation runs on both, triggered via `applyValidations` on before-insert/before-update from the trigger handler), so I gate rule 2 on there being originals (`RecordMap_OLD.size() > 0` equivalent via `getChangedRecords` returning the update pairs). No SOQL needed; everything is bulk-safe via maps.

