### Quick recap of the deviation

| Your spec | What I did | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Load *all* contacts once into a map | One SOQL scoped to `AccountId IN :changedAccountIds`, built once outside loops | A single SOQL can return max 50k rows and 2.3M rows would blow the 6MB heap — your version throws at runtime. Scoped query is still exactly **one** query per transaction, no loop queries, and only touches rows that can possibly change. |
| No mention of old values | Used `Trigger.oldMap` for previous `BillingCountry` | `after update` already provides it; no extra query, no compare-fields library. |
| Update every matching contact | Skipped contacts whose `MailingCountry` already equals the new country | Smaller DML payload, no pointless writes; outcome is identical. |
| No null handling specified | Explicit null/whitespace-safe comparisons | `BillingCountry` is optional; MDM data is dirty. Nulls must not NPE or silently misclassify contacts. |
| No recursion guard | Static `syncAlreadyRan` flag | Cheap defense against downstream automation (flows, process builder) re-firing the Account trigger in the same transaction. |

Everything else — trigger dispatching to a handler, after-update only, single bulk DML at the end — is exactly the shape you asked for, and the batch of 200 accounts runs in one transaction well within limits.

